--TEST--
A `Core\Sse\Message` cannot be constructed, so the only way to one is a `receive()` inside the isolate `Core\Sse::upgrade` opened — `rule:concurrency/two-doors-one-isolate` and `rule:concurrency/a-connection-is-a-loop`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// An event stream's message is what a publisher put on a topic, and a topic is
// the only way two connections meet. A program that could build one out of
// nothing would be forging the delivery rather than receiving it -- and the
// topic it forged is the name the reader below dispatches on. So this case
// pins the other half of that: a `Core`-owned class has no constructor a
// program can call, and this is one of the two where it matters most.
class Feed {
    public static function describe(Core\Sse\Message $msg): string {
        if ($msg->value() != null) {
            return $msg->topic();
        }
        return "nothing was published";
    }
}

var $forged = new Core\Sse\Message();
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0405]: `Core\Sse\Message` has no member named `constructor`
   --> case.nvs:17:15
   |
17 | var $forged = new Core\Sse\Message();
   |               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ referenced here

